hi I'm Dr Anika vascara I'm an OB GYN  generalist with Intermountain Healthcare  in Las Vegas at our Women's Health  clinic and I'm here to talk about  gynecologic cancers today so when we  talk about gynecologic cancers we're  mainly talking about uterine cancer  especially endometrial cancer ovarian  and cervical cancer endometrial cancer  is the most common gynecologic cancer in  the U.S with an estimated 65 000 new  diagnoses per year and about 12 000  deaths from this cancer per year the  average age of diagnosis is over the age  of 60. typically it affects  post-menopausal women but with certain  risk factors can affect younger women  occasionally so risk factors of  endometrial cancer are increasing age  especially postmenopausal obesity  meaning body mass index of greater than  or equal to 30. history of an ovulation  or not ovulating every month some  protective factors are of course  maintaining a healthy lifestyle a BMI as  well as potentially some of the  contraceptives including birth control  pills and specifically the hormonal  intrauterine device or IUD some symptoms  of endometrial cancer are specifically  post-menopausal bleeding because any  type of bleeding out after menopause is  abnormal so definitely let your doctor  know about those symptoms as well as  some other non-specific symptoms like  pelvic pain pain with intercourse or you  know abdominal bloating or swelling so  some risk factors for ovarian cancer our  age with diagnoses rarely happening  before the age of 40. family history or  genetic predisposition to ovarian cancer  obesity not having any prior pregnancies  in the past  as well as infertility and use of  infertility medications for more than a  year  some protective factors for prevention  of ovarian cancer are use of birth  control pills for at least five years as  well as personal history of the removal  of Fallopian tubes and or ovaries for  prior non-cancer indications okay and  last we have cervical cancer so cervical  cancer used to be one of the most common  gynecologic cancers worldwide however  with the start of screening such as the  pap smear as well as HPV or human  papillomavirus vaccination that number  has decreased in the United States  there's about 14 000 new diagnoses  occurring each year with the majority  being in women between the ages of 35  and 44 and about 4 000 deaths from  cervical cancer each year so some risk  factors for cervical cancer the main one  really is human papillomavirus infection  which occurs with skin to skin contact  including sexual contact there are two  there are many different strains of HPV  and the lowest risk types cause genital  warts with the high-risk types being the  ones we are most focused on for cervical  cancer as they are the ones that cause  cervical cancer other risk factors  include not getting your Pap screenings  or HPV screenings regularly or as  directed and also smoking  thank you to Intermountain Healthcare  for sponsoring this segment for more  information head to  intermountainhealthcare.org Nevada we'll  be right back
 

